  ### Deviation hunting

  **1. Seed ordering.** OpenMS: apices = all peaks with intensity > chrom_peak_snr * noise_threshold_int (strict >). stable_sort ascending by intensity, iterate in REVERSE. So descending intensity; ties: reverse of stable ascending order → among equal intensities, the one with the LARGER original index comes first. Original index = order in the (scan, peak) flattened vector. Tool: line 749-752: sorts descending by intensity, ties by `a > b` (larger peak index first). Peak index k in the slab is frame-major, and within a frame sorted by tof (mz). OpenMS flattens by (scan idx, peak idx within spectrum) — peak idx within a spectrum is by m/z. So the tie-break order matches: larger (scan,peak) lexicographic = larger k. Match. ✓

  But — OpenMS apices collection: does it include peaks ≤ noise? Those were DELETED from work_exp before tracing, so no. Tool requires member[k] (inten > noise) AND inten > snr*noise. ✓. Also OpenMS checks `if (!peak_visited[apex_scan_idx][apex_peak_idx])` — skip visited. Tool: `if (visited[seed]) continue;` ✓.

  One subtlety: OpenMS chrom_peak_snr filter — apex must have intensity > chrom_peak_snr * noise_threshold_int. Strict. Tool: `sl.inten[k] > min_apex` with `min_apex = (float)(snr*noise)` — note the float rounding of the threshold: `(float)(snr * noise)` computed in double then cast to float, comparison in float. OpenMS compares double intensity vs double threshold. A peak with intensity exactly at the double threshold could round differently. Negligible — mention as COORD/none? Actually it could flip SET membership for a seed at the exact boundary. Essentially negligible; mention.

